\section{Methods}
\subsection{Hybrid Compute Fabric Scheduling}
SASA-v9.0 employs a deterministic scheduling model in which each compute pillar
executes a specialized subset of the relativistic tensor evolution pipeline.
Let $\mathcal{C}_{\text{GPU}}$, $\mathcal{C}_{\text{WSE}}$, and
$\mathcal{C}_{\text{Q}}$ denote the GPU, wafer-scale, and quantum subsystems,
respectively. The global scheduler enforces:

\[
\mathcal{S}(t) = \{\mathcal{C}_{\text{GPU}}(t),\,
\mathcal{C}_{\text{WSE}}(t),\,
\mathcal{C}_{\text{Q}}(t)\}
\]

subject to the coherence constraint:

\[
\left| t_{\text{GPU}} - t_{\text{Q}} \right| \leq 42\ \text{fs}.
\]

\subsection{Tensor Evolution Pipeline}
The relativistic evolution pipeline is decomposed into:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Metric update
    \item Extrinsic curvature update
    \item Constraint enforcement
    \item Shock-front refinement
\end{enumerate}

Each stage is mapped to the subsystem with optimal throughput. For example,
wafer-scale PDE sweeps execute:

\[
\partial_t \phi = -\frac{1}{6}\alpha K + \beta^i \partial_i \phi
\]

across 900k parallel cores.

\subsection{Quantum-Timed Synchronization}
The DOCCF timing fabric distributes optical clock pulses across all nodes.
Let $\tau_i$ denote the local clock phase of node $i$. DOCCF enforces:

\[
\tau_i - \tau_j = \epsilon_{ij}, \qquad |\epsilon_{ij}| \leq 42\ \text{fs}.
\]

This ensures deterministic ordering of tensor updates across heterogeneous
compute domains.